A forwarded signal handler must bracket itself with interrupt_enter.

The pair was skipped whenever in-guest IPC was on, justified by sigexc having no daemon Mach wait to
clear. But interrupt_enter has a second job: it tells the daemon to hold replies while a handler
runs. Without it, a signal delivered while a call is waiting lets the handler's own calls consume
the reply the interrupted call was waiting for. The request and reply streams then sit one out of
step for the rest of the process's life, and a later call waits for a reply that was already taken.

iTerm2 sets the pty window size after laying out its window, so SIGWINCH reaches login in the middle
of mach_reply_port. The trace showed it exactly: 110 requests against 111 replies, no interrupt_enter
anywhere, and login waiting forever in recvmsg. With the bracket restored the same run counts 110
against 110.

That is the whole claim. login still does not reach the shell, so this removes the skew rather than
the symptom.

--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/signal/sigaction.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/signal/sigaction.c
@@ -175,14 +175,21 @@
 #endif
 
 static void handler_linux_to_bsd_wrapper(int linux_signum, struct linux_siginfo* info, void* ctxt) {
-	// Self-contained (flag-on): interrupt is a no-op -- sigexc_enter has no ciderd Mach wait to clear,
-	// and its user_state is read only by thread_get_state, which a forwarded handler never calls.
-	int inguest = __inguest_ipc_on();
-	if (!inguest)
-		dserver_rpc_interrupt_enter();
+	/*
+	 * THE PAIR IS NOT ONLY ABOUT sigexc, WHICH IS WHY IT USED TO BE SKIPPED WITH IN-GUEST IPC ON.
+	 *
+	 * interrupt_enter also tells the daemon to hold replies while a handler runs. Without it, a
+	 * signal delivered while a call is waiting lets the handler's own calls consume the reply the
+	 * interrupted call was waiting for: the request and reply streams go one out of step and stay
+	 * that way, so a later call waits for a reply that was already taken.
+	 *
+	 * iTerm2 sets the pty window size after laying out its window, SIGWINCH reaches login in the
+	 * middle of mach_reply_port, and login then waits forever and never execs the shell. The trace
+	 * shows it exactly: 110 requests, 111 replies, no interrupt_enter anywhere.
+	 */
+	dserver_rpc_interrupt_enter();
 	handler_linux_to_bsd(linux_signum, info, ctxt);
-	if (!inguest)
-		dserver_rpc_interrupt_exit();
+	dserver_rpc_interrupt_exit();
 };
 
 static int sigflags_bsd_to_linux(int flags)
